1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:06,528
It is Sunday, the day after the step-up, and Aziz opens the mobile bank at breakfast to check his balance.

2
00:00:06,578 --> 00:00:09,360
He opens it again at lunch, and once more at night.

3
00:00:09,410 --> 00:00:11,232
He never types his password.

4
00:00:11,282 --> 00:00:14,760
So what lets him in, again and again, without a login?

5
00:00:14,810 --> 00:00:20,976
Lesson 16 made this phone his money phone, with an app PIN and a binding check at each request.

6
00:00:21,026 --> 00:00:24,144
Now look at what the phone carries between two logins.

7
00:00:24,194 --> 00:00:26,016
It carries an access grant.

8
00:00:26,066 --> 00:00:31,728
An access grant is a session: one app account, on one device, for one customer.

9
00:00:31,778 --> 00:00:36,864
Aziz got his first grant on registration day, the Sunday before, at level one.

10
00:00:36,914 --> 00:00:39,168
He gets a new grant after each login.

11
00:00:39,218 --> 00:00:43,512
Two parts of the platform make a grant, and they have very different jobs.

12
00:00:43,562 --> 00:00:45,552
The customer gateway decides.

13
00:00:45,602 --> 00:00:50,520
It checks the password, the code, the verdict of TAF and the device.

14
00:00:50,570 --> 00:00:52,392
Keycloak only issues.

15
00:00:52,442 --> 00:00:56,280
The app never calls Keycloak, since it talks only to the gateway.

16
00:00:56,330 --> 00:01:00,384
When the gateway is satisfied, it signs a short note for Keycloak.

17
00:01:00,434 --> 00:01:05,448
The note says three things: this device, this customer, this level.

18
00:01:05,498 --> 00:01:09,072
It is valid for a very short time, and only once.

19
00:01:09,122 --> 00:01:15,168
Keycloak reads the note and issues two tokens: an access token and a refresh token.

20
00:01:15,218 --> 00:01:21,168
Keycloak is a well-known engine for tokens, but it knows nothing about TAF or MyID.

21
00:01:21,218 --> 00:01:27,720
So all the rules of the bank stay in the gateway, and a new version of Keycloak never changes a decision.

22
00:01:27,770 --> 00:01:29,736
Now read the access token.

23
00:01:29,786 --> 00:01:35,448
First, it names its app two times: who the token is for, and which app asked for it.

24
00:01:35,498 --> 00:01:37,944
Here, both names say the mobile bank.

25
00:01:37,994 --> 00:01:42,936
The backend of the mobile bank accepts only tokens that were issued for the mobile bank.

26
00:01:42,986 --> 00:01:48,288
A token for any other app gets one answer: session of a different app, log in again.

27
00:01:48,338 --> 00:01:52,440
The app comes from the signed token, never from a header that anyone can write.

28
00:01:52,490 --> 00:01:56,928
Second, the token carries the level of the session: one or two.

29
00:01:56,978 --> 00:02:02,160
The gateway writes level two only for a customer at level two, on his trusted phone.

30
00:02:02,210 --> 00:02:06,504
On registration day, Aziz's token said level one.

31
00:02:06,554 --> 00:02:13,632
On Saturday he passed MyID on this phone, and the gateway issued a new token with level two at once.

32
00:02:13,682 --> 00:02:16,056
He did not wait for the old token to run out.

33
00:02:16,106 --> 00:02:19,464
The token also says whether this phone is the money phone.

34
00:02:19,514 --> 00:02:25,632
So the backend of the mobile bank reads the level and the money phone straight from the token, with no extra call.

35
00:02:25,682 --> 00:02:27,504
Back to breakfast.

36
00:02:27,554 --> 00:02:32,544
The access token lives only for a short, configured time, on purpose.

37
00:02:32,594 --> 00:02:35,664
When it runs out, the app asks Aziz for nothing.

38
00:02:35,714 --> 00:02:40,056
It sends the refresh token to the gateway, with its device identifier.

39
00:02:40,106 --> 00:02:41,928
This is a renewal.

40
00:02:41,978 --> 00:02:45,816
The gateway renews only for the same device and the same app.

41
00:02:45,866 --> 00:02:50,304
First, the renewal passes the binding check from Lesson 16.

42
00:02:50,354 --> 00:02:54,096
Then the gateway looks at its list of ended grants, at each call.

43
00:02:54,146 --> 00:03:00,312
When both checks pass, Keycloak gives a fresh access token, and a fresh refresh token too.

44
00:03:00,362 --> 00:03:05,616
The old refresh token is now used up, and Keycloak sees it if it ever comes back.

45
00:03:05,666 --> 00:03:07,848
A renewal needs no password.

46
00:03:07,898 --> 00:03:12,216
The password opens a login, and a renewal only continues one.

47
00:03:12,266 --> 00:03:16,368
That is also why the password lock of Lesson 11 ends no grant.

48
00:03:16,418 --> 00:03:18,936
There is one more limit: inactivity.

49
00:03:18,986 --> 00:03:24,624
When Aziz leaves the app alone for longer than the configured limit, the app asks for his app PIN.

50
00:03:24,674 --> 00:03:29,424
The grant does not end, and the PIN only unlocks the app on this phone.

51
00:03:29,474 --> 00:03:36,336
The full lifetime of a grant and the inactivity limit are policies, and they come from the installation configuration.

52
00:03:36,386 --> 00:03:39,096
Now the other side: how a grant ends.

53
00:03:39,146 --> 00:03:41,160
First, its lifetime runs out.

54
00:03:41,210 --> 00:03:47,640
Second, the bank blocks the customer, as Lesson 19 shows on the day Aziz loses his phone.

55
00:03:47,690 --> 00:03:52,536
The grant on the phone then ends at its very next request, not at its next renewal.

56
00:03:52,586 --> 00:03:59,040
Third, a binding mismatch: the gateway revokes the device, and its grant ends at that request.

57
00:03:59,090 --> 00:04:03,480
Fourth, a closure of the customer ends every grant he has.

58
00:04:03,530 --> 00:04:07,632
In each case, the app gets the same answer: this grant has ended.

59
00:04:07,682 --> 00:04:12,912
The app tries a renewal, and when the renewal fails too, Aziz must log in again.

60
00:04:12,962 --> 00:04:14,784
Now a check for you.

61
00:04:14,834 --> 00:04:21,648
At lunch, after three quiet hours, what does Aziz type: his password, his app PIN, or nothing?

62
00:04:21,698 --> 00:04:28,680
His app PIN, because the app was idle past the limit, while the grant goes on and its renewal needs no password.

63
00:04:28,730 --> 00:04:31,848
The gateway decides, and Keycloak issues.

64
00:04:31,898 --> 00:04:34,680
The token names its app and carries the level.

65
00:04:34,730 --> 00:04:37,992
A renewal keeps the session alive with no password.

66
00:04:38,042 --> 00:04:41,280
And an ended grant stops at the very next request.

67
00:04:41,330 --> 00:04:48,936
On Monday his nephew wants the phone, so Aziz logs out, and Lesson 18 shows how he logs in again on the same phone.
